August 11, 2003

Sauce's Visual VU Debugger (SVVUDB) Alpha Release
-------------------------------------------------

This is an alpha pre-release of SVVUDB - Sauce's Visual VU Debugger.

This document will serve as the user manual until the feature set has
been frozen, allowing a more formal manual to be developed.  It is,
after all, very difficult and cumbersome to document a moving target.

This new version adds clients for windows and PC Linux

Contents:
   I. SVVUDB IS *NOT JUST* AN X-WINDOWS APPLICATION!!!
  II. INVOKING SVVUDB
 III. THE SVVUDB INTERFACE
  IV. ADDING BREAKPOINTS
   V. GRAPHICS CONTEXTS
  VI. DEBUGGING WITH THE VU CODING CONTEST HARNESS
 VII. DEBUGGING REMOTELY (Windows, PC Linux or other PS2)
VIII. USING SVVUDB ON WINDOWS

I. SVVUDB IS *NOT JUST* AN X-WINDOWS APPLICATION!!!
---------------------------------------------------
   The latest version comes with a client for Windows as well as a
client for PC Linux.

SVVUDB can be used to debug VU0 and VU1 code.

II. INVOKING SVVUDB
-------------------

Invoking "./svvudb -h" will yield output that describes the command
line parameters similar to this:

./svvudb [-vu0 | -vu1] [-bp <address>] [-c <address>,<file>]
         [-d <address>,<file>] [-s <address>]
        Where <address> is a decimal address
        or a hex address in the format of 0x123
        and <file> is a filename.
        -bp adds a breakpoint at <address>
        -c uploads code from <file> to <address>
        -d uploads data from <file> to <address>
        Note that multiple bp, -c, and -d directives can be specified.
        -s starts the VU at <address> if it is not already running.
        Note also that if -vu0 or -vu1 are not specified, -vu1 is
        assumed

The -vu0 | -vu1 Option
----------------------
-vu0 or -vu1 can be specified to indicate the VU that the debugger
should target.  -vu1 is assumed if neither is specified.  You cannot
specify both at the same time.

The -bp <address> Option
------------------------
The -bp option can be used multiple times; it is used to add a
breakpoint to VU code that is already loaded or code that is being
loaded from the command line.  The address, in 64 bit increments, can
be written in decimal or hexadecimal, so:

   -bp 16

and

   -bp 0x10

Are the same.  They will set a breakpoint at the seventeenth (17th)
instruction in the VU code memory.

The -c <address>,<file> Option
------------------------------
The -c option can be used to upload a microprogram.  As with the -bp
option, the address can be specified in hexadecimal or in decimal.  To
upload test.bin to address 0x10, invoke:

   -c 0x10,test.bin

Note the space after the -c, and no spaces before or after the comma.
You can specify multiple -c options.

The -d <address>,<file> Option
------------------------------
The -d option can be used to upload a data.  As with the -bp option,
the address can be specified in hexadecimal or in decimal.  Unlike the
-bp option, however the address specifies QWORD increments.  To upload
test.dat to address 0x10, invoke:

   -d 0x10,test.dat

Note the space after the -d, and no spaces before or after the comma.
You can specify multiple -d options.

The -s <address> Option
-----------------------
Being able to upload code and data is only useful if you can actually
cause the debugger to execute code (on the other hand, the debugger
can be used on code driven by other applications simply by setting
breakpoints).  The -s switch is used to specify the starting address
of the VU microprogram.  This is from which execution will start.  So,
to put it all together:

   ./svvudb -bp 0x10 -s 0x10 -c 0x10,test.bin -d 0x10,test.dat

Will upload the code file test.bin to micro memory address 0x10, the
data file test.dat to data memory address 0x10, set a breakpoint at
0x10 (which is the first instruction of the uploaded code), and begin
execution at that first instruction.

III. THE SVVUDB INTERFACE
-------------------------
   SVVUDB's interface is split up into four windows:
 - The Code Window, which shows a disassembly of the VU code and
 contains the primary interface controls
 - The Memory Window, which shows the VU data memory and allows you to
 update it
 - The Floating Point Register Window, which shows the 32 vector
 registers VF00-VF31 and allows you to change their values, and
 - The Integer/Other Register Window which shows the scalar registers
 VI00-VI15, as well as special registers which include STATUS, MAC,
 CLIP, R, I, P, Q and TPC.  Most of these registers can be edited as
 well.

   As you step through your code, the memory and register windows will
highlight all values that have changed since the last instruction.

To Step Through Your Code
-------------------------
 - Use the "Step" button or press s to single-step through your code.
 - Use the "Run" button or press r to run until the next breakpoint
   has reached.

To Add/Remove a Breakpoint
--------------------------
   Ensure the instruction is selected and press the "Add Breakpoint"
button or "Clear Breakpoint" button.  You may also use the keyboard
shortcuts 'b' or 'x'.

To Edit Memory or a Register
----------------------------
    Right-click on the memory or register you would like to edit.  You
may edit the value and press "Ok" to commit the changes, or "Cancel"
to ignore them.  All hexadecimal values must be written as "0x123".

IV. ADDING BREAKPOINTS
----------------------
   SVVUDB's GUI (graphical user interface) becomes active only after a
breakpoint has been triggered.  SVVUDB offers many ways to set
breakpoints in your code.

1. Adding Breakpoints through the Command Line
----------------------------------------------
   The simplest way to set a breakpoint within SVVUDB is through the
command line.  This assumes that the VU code has already been uploaded
by the time the debugger is started.  It requires no change in the
application, however, assumes that you will not overwrite the VU micro
memory with new code after adding this breakpoint.

   As an example:

./svvudb -bp 0 -vu1

will add a breakpoint at address 0 in VU1.

2. Embedding Breakpoints in VU Source
-------------------------------------
   Breakpoints can be embedded into VU source files by setting the D
bit.  This is a good choice for developers who do not want to touch
their application source at all but don't mind changing the VU source.
This will also allow the developer to understand right away how the
breakpoint corresponds to the VU source -- since the developer places
it in the appropriate spot directly in the VU source.  The following
is an example of a NOP instruction that will trigger the debugger.

nop[D]		nop

3. Dynamically Inserting Breakpoints
------------------------------------
   By dynamically inserting breakpoints, a developer can truly
leverage SVVUDB's ability to run an application uninterrupted until a
breakpoint is reached.  This approach is particularly useful for
applications that may upload multiple VU microprograms in a session
but the developer needs to debug only one of them.

   You must compute the address of the instruction at which to break.
Each VU instruction takes up 64 bits, and bit 60 (counting from zero)
is the breakpoint bit, which must be set.  So in order to add a
breakpoint in the third instruction:

sps2uint64 *pCode=(sps2uint64) myCodePointer;
pCode[2] |= (sps2uint64) 1 << 60;

   (instead of sps2uint64 you may use your own flavor of a unsigned 64
   bit integer type).

   This will allow you to set a breakpoint dynamically in your code
either before transferring it to VU micro memory (or after you
transfer it if you memory map your VU code).

V. GRAPHICS CONTEXTS
--------------------
   SVVUDB allows you to configure the portion of the GS memory that is
being displayed.  This is particularly useful since many applications
are double-buffered; they draw one on buffer as they display the
other.  Unfortunately, as you debug, it often is more useful to see
the image being drawn; however, most applications will only show the
previously completed frame.  This is where SVVUDB's Graphics Contexts
come in.

   You must first ensure that two graphics contexts have been properly
configured to correspond to the two buffers in your double-buffered
application.  This is done by pressing the "Graphics Context Settings"
button in the code window.  From the drop-down menu at the top, there
are a number of pre-configured settings from the file svvudb.gc.txt.
You may add additional settings to that file, or you may edit the
values as they appear on the screen.

   Once properly configured, you will be able to quickly toggle
between the two buffers by clicking on GS Context 1 and GS Context 2
(or pressing '1' or '2').  This will allow you to quickly switch
between the frame being drawn and the last completed frame.

   In order to add an entry to svvudb.gc.txt, it is recommended that
you clone an existing entry and adjust the values as appropriate.  The
values are basically a list of the fields for the corresponding
registers, from right to left (i.e. least significant bits come
first).  

IMPORTANT HINT: The first entry in svvudb.gc.txt define the settings
used by default.

VI. DEBUGGING WITH THE VU CODING CONTEST HARNESS
------------------------------------------------
   Debugging with the VU coding contest harness is easy!

   1. Start up the harness
   2. Start your demo
   3. Run "./svvudb -bp 0"
   4. Click on "Graphics Context Settings" and load the preset that
      matches the command line you use for the harness

And you're off!  "./svvudb -bp 0" loads the debugger, setting a
breakpoint at address 0 which is the starting address of coding
contest entries.

VII. DEBUGGING REMOTELY (Windows, PC Linux or other PS2)
--------------------------------------------------------
You can now debug from a remote machine.  On the PS2 to be debugged,
start svvudb in server mode by running:

svvudb -server

If the port is NOT 6123, then make note of it.  On the client machine,
run:

svvudb -client <server host> [-port <server port>] [other arguments]

All other arguments stay the same.  Note that on a PC linux system
you'll have to run svvudb-pc-linux.  You only need to specify the port
if you did not bind the server port to 6123.

VIII. USING SVVUDB ON WINDOWS
-----------------------------

If you want to use the windows version of SVVUDB, you will need to get
the GTK+ runtime files from www.dropline.net/gtk/ and install them.

Also, since the *.dll files will NOT be in your path, you will need to
add them before running SVVUDB.
